  q(BIO,'What is the main ethical concern with genetic testing of minors for adult-onset diseases?','It removes their choice to know',['It is always too expensive','It cannot detect any mutations','It requires surgery to perform'],'A','Experts advise waiting until adulthood unless testing changes care in childhood.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which case involved a young woman in a persistent vegetative state and the right of her family to remove a ventilator?','In re Quinlan',['Roe v. Wade','Griswold v. Connecticut','Baby K case'],'B','The 1976 New Jersey ruling supported surrogate decision-making and ethics committees.',FREM),
  q(BIO,'The Cruzan case (1990) established which requirement in some states?','Clear proof of the patient’s wishes',['Approval from two separate courts','Consent from every family member','A waiting period of one full year'],'C','Missouri could require clear and convincing evidence before stopping nutrition and hydration.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'What is the principle of double effect?','A good act may have a foreseen bad side effect',['Two doctors must agree on every care plan','Patients must consent to care twice','Every treatment has two equal risks'],'D','For example, high-dose pain relief may hasten death if the intent is only comfort.',FREM),
  q(BIO,'Which statement describes palliative care?','Comfort care at any illness stage',['Care only for the last 72 hours of life','Treatment aimed only at a cure','Care given only in a hospice building'],'A','Hospice is palliative care for patients with a prognosis of 6 months or less.',RIT),
  q(BIO,'What is the purpose of an institutional review board (IRB)?','To protect human research subjects',['To approve hospital budget plans','To license physicians in a state','To manage organ waiting lists'],'B','IRBs review research for risks, benefits and informed consent.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'What does the term “medical futility” describe?','Care unlikely to help the patient',['Care paid for only by the patient','A treatment banned by federal law','Any procedure done by a resident'],'C','Physicians are not required to provide care that offers no reasonable benefit.',FREM),
  q(BIO,'Which act allows people to donate organs by signing a donor card or registry?','Uniform Anatomical Gift Act',['National Organ Sales Act','Patient Self-Determination Act','Health Care Quality Act'],'D','The National Organ Transplant Act of 1984 prohibits buying and selling organs.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Henrietta Lacks’s cells were used without her knowledge. Which ethical principle was most clearly violated?','Informed consent',['Justice in organ sharing','Beneficence in surgery','Fidelity to an employer'],'A','Her HeLa cells became a key research line, raising issues of consent and benefit sharing.',RIT),
  q(BIO,'What is a major ethical concern about xenotransplantation?','Transmitting animal viruses to humans',['It uses only human organ donors','It is required by federal law','It removes the need for any drugs'],'B','Pig organs could carry viruses; animal welfare is also a concern.',FREM),
  q(BIO,'Which statement about CRISPR germline editing is correct?','Changes can pass to future generations',['Changes affect only a single organ','It is approved for routine use in the U.S.','It cannot change DNA sequences'],'C','Germline edits affect eggs, sperm or embryos, so they are inherited.',TAM),
  q(BIO,'Which ethical issue is most tied to the allocation of scarce organs?','Distributive justice',['Patient fidelity','Veracity in records','Professional courtesy'],'D','Matching criteria weigh need, compatibility, wait time and likely benefit.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'A medical assistant views a celebrity patient’s chart out of curiosity. What has occurred?','A HIPAA privacy violation',['An approved privacy audit','A routine workflow','A legal disclosure'],'A','Access is limited to what is needed for treatment, payment or operations.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'How long does HIPAA give a covered entity to act on a patient’s request for access to their records?','30 days',['24 hours','7 days','90 days'],'B','One 30-day extension is allowed with written notice to the patient.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which entity is a business associate under HIPAA?','A billing company for claims',['A patient’s family member','The patient’s employer','A public library user'],'C','Business associates must sign agreements to protect health information.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Which law protects the confidentiality of substance use disorder treatment records with stricter rules than HIPAA?','42 CFR Part 2',['Title IX','The Stark Law','OSHA Standards'],'D','Part 2 programs generally need specific written consent before disclosure.',TAM),
  q(HCE,'Which law prohibits physicians from referring Medicare patients to entities in which they have a financial interest?','The Stark Law',['The HITECH Act','The ADA','The FMLA'],'A','Stark is a strict liability law, so intent does not have to be proven.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Which agency enforces the HIPAA Privacy Rule?','Office for Civil Rights',['The Joint Commission','The FDA','The DEA'],'B','OCR, part of HHS, investigates complaints and can impose fines.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which law allows workers to keep group health insurance for a time after losing a job?','COBRA',['ERISA only','CLIA','GINA'],'C','COBRA coverage usually lasts 18 months, and the worker pays the full premium.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'A worker is injured on the job. What usually covers medical costs and lost wages?','Workers’ compensation',['Medicare Part D coverage','Medicaid','Social Security'],'D','Workers’ comp is no-fault insurance, and workers usually cannot sue the employer.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which law requires labs that test human specimens to meet quality standards?','CLIA',['COBRA','EMTALA','ERISA'],'A','The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments of 1988 set lab certification rules.',TAM),
  q(HCE,'Which is an example of a reportable event in most states?','A gunshot wound',['A routine sore throat','A sprained ankle','A seasonal allergy'],'B','States also require reporting of births, deaths, abuse and certain diseases.',TAM),
  q(HCE,'What is the purpose of an incident report?','To document an event for safety',['To discipline the employee involved','To place in the patient’s chart','To file a lawsuit against staff'],'C','Incident reports are kept out of the chart and help prevent future errors.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A provider tells a patient that a test result is normal when it is not, to avoid upsetting her. Which principle is violated?','Veracity',['Justice','Fidelity','Nonmaleficence only'],'D','Veracity is the duty to tell the truth so patients can make informed choices.',TAM),
  q(HCE,'Which law prohibits age discrimination against workers aged 40 and older?','The ADEA',['The FLSA','The NLRA','The ERISA'],'A','The Age Discrimination in Employment Act applies to employers with 20 or more workers.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which item is required for a valid HIPAA authorization to release records?','An expiration date or event',['A notarized seal on the form','The physician’s home address','A copy of the patient’s bill'],'B','Authorizations must also describe the information, the recipient and the purpose.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'Who may legally access a deceased patient’s records under HIPAA?','The executor of the estate',['Any friend who visited the patient','Any reporter who files a request','Any employee in the hospital'],'C','Protections last 50 years after death; the executor acts for the patient.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which act sets minimum wage and overtime rules for many healthcare workers?','Fair Labor Standards Act',['National Labor Relations Act','Occupational Safety Act','Equal Pay Act of 1963'],'D','Most hourly employees must receive 1.5 times their rate for hours over 40 per week.',RIT),
  q(HCE,'Which practice best protects patient privacy at a front desk?','Using a sign-in sheet without diagnoses',['Calling out the patient’s diagnosis','Leaving charts open on the counter','Discussing cases in the lobby'],'A','Incidental disclosures are allowed only when reasonable safeguards are in place.',FREM),
  q(HCE,'A nurse discovers a coworker diverting opioids. What is the most appropriate first action?','Report it through the chain of command',['Ignore it to protect the coworker','Confront the coworker in public','Post about it on social media'],'B','Reporting protects patients; many states offer peer assistance programs for impaired workers.',TAM)
